      "id": "comparison-tsla-ford-strategy",
      "category": "cross_company_comparison",
      "query": "Compare Tesla's autonomous service strategy with Ford Model e's technology focus.",
      "answer": "Tesla’s autonomous service strategy centers on operating its own ride-hailing network, while Ford Model e’s focus is on EV and connected-vehicle technology development rather than a specific autonomous service.\n\n- **Tesla**: In June 2025 Tesla launched a **Robotaxi service**, described as “an autonomous ride-hailing platform that harnesses our technology and vehicles.” Tesla states that this service, together with products such as **FSD (Supervised) subscriptions**, is intended to “advance a service-driven business model based on AI, software and fleet-based profits.” The Robotaxi business currently uses **Model Y** vehicles and is expected to incorporate **Cybercab**, a purpose-built autonomous vehicle, over time. [TSLA-2025-CHUNK-000003]\n\n- **Ford Model e**: The Ford Model e segment is defined around **sale of EVs (including EREVs), service parts, accessories, and digital services**, and it “focuses on developing EV and digital vehicle technologies, as well as software development.” It also “provides software and connected vehicle technologies on behalf of the enterprise” and manufactures certain EVs (including for Ford Pro), with operations in North America, Europe, and China. [F-2025-CHUNK-000005]\n\nBased on these disclosures, Tesla’s strategy emphasizes an **autonomous ride-hailing service and AI/software-driven fleet business**, whereas Ford Model e emphasizes **development and provision of EV, digital, and connected-vehicle technologies and software** without a specifically described autonomous ride-hailing service in the available evidence. [TSLA-2025-CHUNK-000003][F-2025-CHUNK-000005]",
